<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Squad on ACA — Microsoft Agent Framework adapter.

    THE QUARANTINE IS THE POINT. Squad.Aca.Agents (the contract) has zero
    PackageReference elements and scripts/validate.ps1 asserts it. Every
    third-party dependency the agent story needs lives HERE instead, so a
    restore failure, a breaking API change or a package yank in the Agent
    Framework can only take this project down — not the contract, not the
    AppHost, and not the control plane.

    The dependency flows one way and only one way:

        Squad.Aca.Agents.MAF  ->  Squad.Aca.Agents      (project reference)
        Squad.Aca.Agents      ->  (nothing)

    VERSION PINNING. Microsoft.Agents.AI is pinned to an exact version, not a
    floating range. A floating "1.*" would let a routine `dotnet restore` change
    the compiled surface of a shipped adapter with no diff to review, which is
    precisely the instability this quarantine exists to contain. Bumping it is a
    deliberate, reviewable edit here.

    VERSION VALIDATED: 1.16.0.

    A NOTE ON "PREVIEW". The original AgentAbstraction.cs header said the Agent
    Framework packages "are still preview and their surface changes between
    releases". That was true when it was written; as of this sprint
    Microsoft.Agents.AI ships STABLE — 1.0.0 GA, then 1.1.0 through 1.16.0.
    The quarantine is kept anyway. Its value was never solely "preview": a caller
    who wants only the contract should not be forced to restore
    Microsoft.Extensions.AI, Microsoft.ML.Tokenizers and their transitive graph
    to get it.

    TARGET FRAMEWORK — net9.0, matching Squad.Aca.Agents. Microsoft.Agents.AI
    ships netstandard2.0, net472, net8.0, net9.0 and net10.0 assets, so net9.0 is
    a first-class target for it and needs no SDK bump; CI provisions 9.0.x.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <RootNamespace>Squad.Aca.Agents.MAF</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Agents.AI" Version="1.16.0" />

    <!--
      Declared explicitly, at exactly the version Microsoft.Agents.AI 1.16.0
      already brings in transitively. AddSquadAcaAgent() is a public API over
      IServiceCollection, so this project compiles against that assembly whether
      or not the Agent Framework keeps depending on it; leaning on a transitive
      reference for a type in our own public signature is how a minor upstream
      dependency trim becomes our compile break. Pinning it BELOW the transitive
      version fails restore outright (NU1605 under TreatWarningsAsErrors), so
      the two are kept in lockstep by construction.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="10.0.9" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Squad.Aca.Agents\Squad.Aca.Agents.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
